## 5. Phased Build Plan

### Phase 1 — Quick Win MVP 🚀
**Goal**: Ship a polished, usable v1 that looks professional and handles the 80% use case (send one file to someone on the same network).

**Timeline estimate**: 1-2 days

| Feature | Priority | Details |
|---|---|---|
| **Modern, responsive UI** | P0 | Dark theme, glassmorphism, smooth animations, mobile-first layout. Replace the raw prototype HTML. |
| **Drag-and-drop file selection** | P0 | Drop zone with visual feedback + fallback file picker |
| **QR code for room joining** | P0 | Generate QR code from room URL so mobile devices can scan to join |
| **Auto-generated room codes** | P1 | "Create Room" button that generates a random 6-char code instead of manual entry |
| **Visual transfer progress** | P1 | Animated progress bar (not just text %). Keep the speed meter. |
| **Connection state indicators** | P1 | Clear visual states: Disconnected → Connecting → Connected → Transferring → Complete |
| **Responsive mobile layout** | P1 | Works well on phone screens |
| **Basic error handling** | P1 | Graceful messages for: file too large for memory, peer disconnected, WebRTC unavailable |

**Tech choices for Phase 1**:
- Keep Express + Socket.io signaling server (your current stack)
- Keep raw WebRTC (no need for PeerJS/simple-peer yet — your implementation is already solid)
- Add [qrcode.js](https://github.com/davidshimjs/qrcodejs) for QR generation
- Vanilla HTML/CSS/JS (no framework needed yet)

---

### Phase 2 — Usability & Resilience 🛡️
**Goal**: Handle real-world network conditions and support common multi-file workflows.

**Timeline estimate**: 3-5 days

| Feature | Priority | Details |
|---|---|---|
| **TURN server integration** | P0 | Add a TURN server config (e.g., Metered.ca free tier or self-hosted coturn) so transfers work across NATs |
| **Multi-file transfer** | P0 | Select multiple files, send them sequentially or bundle as ZIP using zip.js |
| **Text/clipboard sharing** | P1 | Quick text snippet or clipboard content share alongside files |
| **Receive confirmation dialog** | P1 | Receiver approves/rejects incoming file before transfer starts (like PairDrop) |
| **Transfer history** | P1 | Show recent transfers with download links (stored in IndexedDB) |
| **Reconnection logic** | P2 | Auto-retry WebRTC connection on transient failures |
| **PWA manifest** | P2 | Add manifest.json + service worker for "Add to Home Screen" support |

**Tech additions for Phase 2**:
- [zip.js](https://gildas-lormeau.github.io/zip.js/) for client-side ZIP bundling
- IndexedDB for transfer history & large file chunk storage
- TURN server (coturn self-hosted or Metered.ca API)

---

### Phase 3 — Power Features ⚡
**Goal**: Differentiate from PairDrop/FilePizza with advanced capabilities.

**Timeline estimate**: 1-2 weeks

| Feature | Priority | Details |
|---|---|---|
| **LAN auto-discovery** | P1 | Use the server to group devices by IP subnet — show "Nearby Devices" without a room code |
| **Persistent device pairing** | P1 | Pair devices once (via QR/code), remember them across sessions using shared secrets |
| **Transfer resume** | P1 | Track received bytes in IndexedDB, negotiate resume offset on reconnect |
| **Password-protected rooms** | P2 | Optional password before joining a room |
| **Shareable transfer link** | P2 | Public URL like `fileshare.app/r/ABC123` that anyone can open to connect |
| **Folder transfer** | P2 | Use `webkitdirectory` API to select entire folders |
| **End-to-end encryption** | P2 | Application-level encryption (AES-GCM) on top of DTLS |
| **MCP server** | P3 | Expose FileShare as an MCP tool so AI agents can orchestrate file transfers |

---

## 6. Technology Decisions

> [!IMPORTANT]  
> **Key Decision: Build vs. Adopt a library like PeerJS?**
> 
> Your current raw WebRTC implementation with manual signaling is **already more sophisticated** than what PeerJS would give you (you have adaptive buffering with HIGH_WATER/LOW_WATER thresholds). I recommend **keeping your custom implementation** and adding features incrementally rather than rewriting on top of PeerJS. This gives you full control over performance tuning, which is your differentiator.

### Recommended Stack (All Phases)

| Layer | Technology | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| **Server** | Node.js + Express + Socket.io | Already working, minimal and fast |
| **Frontend** | Vanilla HTML/CSS/JS | No framework overhead for a single-page tool |
| **WebRTC** | Raw `RTCPeerConnection` API | You already have a custom, high-performance implementation |
| **QR Codes** | qrcode.js (CDN) | Lightweight, no build step |
| **ZIP** | zip.js (CDN) | Client-side, streaming ZIP creation |
| **Storage** | IndexedDB (via idb wrapper) | Transfer history, large file chunk buffering |
| **TURN** | coturn (self-hosted) or Metered.ca | NAT traversal for production use |

---

## 7. Success Metrics (Phase 1)

| Metric | Target |
|---|---|
| Time from page load to first file sent | < 30 seconds |
| Transfer speed on LAN | > 5 MB/s |
| Works on mobile Chrome + Safari | ✅ |
| Lighthouse Performance score | > 90 |
| Zero-install experience | ✅ (browser only) |

